{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2003 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "46a7cbe5711fe422d92bdbc36a679bd38a6a0c7d7f4bc52ccf61eae0816ce673" :edb/db-before-t 2002 :edb/db-after-t 2003 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535315 50 #inst "2026-10-13T17:24:47.784Z" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186100888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186494077 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186100888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186494079 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186100888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186494086 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186100888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186494088 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186100888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186494099 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186100888 1040 #edb/ref 17592186494124 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494074 1080 "true" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494074 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494074 1083 #uuid "011418b9-1534-59df-abe2-f6a8f8a26db8" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494075 1080 "false" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494075 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494075 1083 #uuid "021de50f-ea33-579e-a44c-452e1f743ed0" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494076 1080 "true" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494076 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494076 1083 #uuid "03474253-5656-59c7-960b-e9aba91c42ea" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494089 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494102 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494107 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494077 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{3y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-2{x}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=-5{x}^{2}$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494077 1072 #uuid "03ad73f2-dfe8-5b7e-bde4-05067dff734f" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494077 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-2{x}^{3},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-2{x}^{3})}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -5{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-2{x}^{3}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6{x}^{2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3y}{x} \\\\ -6{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3(-2{x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ -6{x}^{2} & =-6{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-2{x}^{3}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494078 1080 "false" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494078 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494078 1083 #uuid "0b03c25e-572f-53bd-97e2-15a9854478d8" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494083 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494121 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494123 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494079 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=-7{x}^{3}$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494079 1072 #uuid "0e664db5-8912-5df5-8f6f-f040a4a2e7d5" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494079 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{4})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-12{x}^{3} \\\\  & \\ne -7{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{4}$ and ${y}^{′}=-12{x}^{3}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ -12{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(-3{x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ -12{x}^{3} & =-12{x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{4}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494080 1080 "false" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494080 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494080 1083 #uuid "0ea9441a-4d5f-5c20-89eb-3e9b8023d358" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494081 1080 "false" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494081 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494081 1083 #uuid "0ed271bf-d9