x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494086 1072 #uuid "22250596-3a65-57bc-811f-38c308a05ad0" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494086 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-4{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-4{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-8x \\\\  & \\ne -6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-4{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-8x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -8x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-4{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -8x & =-8x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-4{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494087 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494085 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494087 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494119 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494087 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408166 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494087 1077 "II" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494087 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494085 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494087 1079 #uuid "22e46930-92a0-561f-be02-0e67f2899630" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494091 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494092 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494117 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494088 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-5{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=-7x$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494088 1072 #uuid "259e28d6-9a87-555f-961a-c712d1726ac4" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494088 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-5{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-5{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-10x \\\\  & \\ne -7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-5{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-10x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -10x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-5{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -10x & =-10x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-5{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494089 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494118 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494089 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494120 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494089 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408166 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494089 1077 "II" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494089 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494120 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494089 1079 #uuid "276d5850-899a-5418-b118-58774ff396c5" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494090 1075